    "title": {
     "en": "The juice challenge"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "A shop poster says: drink 6 glasses of juice and get your money back! Chintu said, “I cannot drink 6 glasses!” because the glasses are big."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "litre",
     "kilogram",
     "capacity",
     "weight"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Welcome to Filling and Lifting. Chintu sees a juice challenge, and the glasses are very big.\nIf the glass is bigger, you drink fewer glasses. If the glass is smaller, you drink more glasses to finish the same drink.\nThe amount a container can hold is called its capacity. We measure the capacity of liquids in litres.\nThe heaviness of an object is its weight. We measure weight in kilograms.\nA bottle of 1 litre milk holds ten glasses of 100 ml (additional, not in the textbook).\nA packet of 1 kilogram sugar is the weight you can weigh against a standard weight."
    }
